  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThe 08Z/3 am surface analysis shows 1035 mb high pressure over\nthe upper Ohio river valley (southeast Ohio, western PA and\nnorthern WV) and ridging sw into the mid MS river valley\nincluding southeast IL. Nearby high pressure ridge providing\nclear skies across IL, with light south to SE winds in central\nIL and near calm winds in southeast IL. Temperatures were in the\nmid 30s to around 40F at 3 am, with Robinson, Marshall and Paris\nclose to freezing. Dewpoints were in the low to mid 30s and \nthis is how cool some areas will likely get by dawn east of the \nIL river valley. Will continue the Frost Advisory until 9 am for\ncentral/eastern CWA from McLean, Logan and Sangamon counties \neast where frost will be more widespread, while patchy frost \nover the IL river valley.\n\nHigh pressure to settle over New England and the mid Atlantic\nStates by sunset and continue to ridge back into IL through\nMonday. Southerly winds increase some today especially\nnorthern/nw CWA with gusts 15-20 mph by afternoon. Ample\nsunshine today with nearby surface high pressure along with\nmid/upper level ridge over IL (500 mb heights over 585\ndecameters). Milder and seasonable highs in the mid to upper \n60s. Seasonably cool night ahead tonight with lows in the upper \n30s to around 40F, with mid 30s possible near the IN border. \nPatchy frost possible in eastern IL later tonight and early Mon \nmorning, though less coverage than early this morning. \nHigh/cirrus clouds to increase from the west late Mon morning \ninto Monday afternoon, though still a fair amount of sunshine. \nDry atmosphere continues to modify with highs Monday in the \nupper 60s and lower 70s. Lows Mon night in the upper 30s and \nlower 40s, coolest in eastern IL.\n\nAnother large high pressure system moves eastward from Ontario\nacross Quebec Province on Tue/Wed while ridging into IL and\ncontinues fair/dry weather. Highs 70-75F on Tue and a few\ndegrees warmer Wed, in the mid 70s. Lows Tue night in the low to\nmid 40s, coolest in eastern IL again. Yet another high pressure\nsystem moves eastward over the northern Great Lakes Thu and \ninto southeast Canada and New England late this week while \nridging toward IL. Meanwhile have strong mid/upper level ridge \nbuilding over IL late this week. These two features to ensure \ndry weather with warmer temperatures with highs in the mid 70s \nto lower 80s from Thu through next Sunday. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 6-10 Day Outlook for Oct \n23-27th has a 65-75% chance of above normal temperatures over \ncentral and southeast IL, with a 40-50% chance of below normal \nprecipitation. This bodes well for the fall harvesting which is \nmore than half way complete over Illinois. CPC 8-14 Day Outlook \nfor Oct 25-31st has a 60-70% chance of above normal temperatures\nand precipitation trending near normal, except trending \nslightly below normal (33-40%) near the Indiana border.",
